<activity>
 <!--Question 10-->
<statement>
<p>If we were to repeat the process in #9 thousands of times, predict the behavior of the distribution of sample means—the graph all the way to the right in the applet. (Shape? Center? Variability? Compared to the population?) Sketch your prediction, changing the horizontal axis scale if necessary.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    We might predict that the graph of sample means would be fairly bell-shaped and symmetric,
    centered near 4.295 and have a smaller standard deviation than 2.119, the standard deviation of
    words in the population.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity> 
 <!--Question 11-->
 <introduction>
  <p>Change the Number of samples from 1 to 9999 (for 10,000 samples) and press Draw Samples.</p>
 </introduction>
<task>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Create a sketch of the distribution in the box below — including carefully labeling the horizontal
      axis and indicating what each dot on the graph represents. (Hint: How many dots do you have? What
      would you/the applet do to add one more dot to the graph?)
    </p>
    <image source="3-2/question-11-a.png" width="100%">
      <description>An empty graph for plotting the sample means (x-axis from 0 to 1.0) and counts
      (y-axis from 0 to 20). </description>
    </image>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      There are 10,000 dots, each represents the mean number of letters of a sample of 5
      words. To add another dot, one must take another sample of 5 words and calculate the
      average number of letters per word.
    </p>
  </solution>
</task>
<task>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Describe the shape of the distribution of sample means. How does it compare to the shape of the population?
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      The distribution of sample means is less variable and more symmetric (though still
      slightly skewed right) compared to the population distribution of word lengths.
    </p>
  </solution>
</task>
<task>
  <statement>
    <p>
      What is the mean of the sampling distribution of sample means? How does it
      compare to the mean of the population?
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      4.305, it is very close to the population mean of 4.295.
    </p>
  </solution>
</task>
<task>
  <statement>
    <p>
      What is the standard deviation of the sampling distribution of sample means? How does it compare to the standard deviation of the population?
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      0.948, it is much smaller than the standard deviation of the population.
    </p>
    <image source="3-2/exercise-11-d.png">
    </image>
  </solution>
</task>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 12-->
<statement>
<p>Does simple random sampling for quantitative data appear to be an unbiased sampling method? How are you deciding?</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    It does appear to be unbiased as the mean of the sample statistics is very close to the population mean
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 13-->
<statement>
<p>Suppose we wanted less sample-to-sample variation in the sample means. What would you change about the sampling process?</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Increase the sample size.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<remark> 
<p>When taking random samples from a large population (more than 20 times the size of
the sample), the distribution of sample means will</p>
<p>
<ol>
<li>Center around the population mean (<m>\mu</m>)</li>
<li>Have a standard deviation smaller than the population,
  <m>SD(\bar{X})=\dfrac{\text{population standard deviation}}{\sqrt{\text{sample size}}}=\dfrac{\sigma}{\sqrt{n}}</m></li>
<li>Be approximately normal when the sample size is large (by the <term>Central Limit Theorem</term> or if
  the population distribution is normally distributed)</li>
</ol>
</p>
</remark>

<p>Like with proportions, we see that the sample mean (<m>\bar{x}</m>) is unbiased for estimating the population mean, but now we have a different formula for predicting the standard deviation of the statistic.</p>

<activity>
 <!--Question 14-->
<statement>
<p>Use your applet output to verify that the standard deviation of sample means is
approximately equal to <m>\sigma/\sqrt{n}</m></p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    <m>\frac{2.119}{\sqrt{5}} \approx 0.948</m> which is the same as the standard deviation of the distribution of sample
    means.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<p>The Central Limit Theorem tells us that we can also assume that the distribution of sample
means is approximately normal when the sample size is large. How large is large? We cannot check
the number of successes and failures because we are working with a quantitative variable. But in
a similar manner, the sampling distribution will look normal as long as the population distribution
is not too skewed. As we increase the sample size, the distribution of sample means will look more
and more like a normal distribution. Generally, if the sample size is at least 20 we can assume
that distribution of sample means is approximately normal. We also have a new case—the distribution
of sample means will behave like a normal distribution for any sample size, if the population shape
is itself approximately normal.</p>

<activity>
 <!--Question 15-->
<statement>
<p>Next you will change the sample size from 5 to 10. Predict how this will impact the shape, center,
and variability of the distribution of sample means.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    We might predict that the shape will be more symmetric, the center will remain close to 4.295,
    and the standard deviation will decrease.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 16-->
<statement>
<p>Generate 10,000 random samples of 10 words. Sketch the resulting distribution
of sample means, remembering to carefully label the horizontal axis. Were your predictions
about how the increased sample size would impact the shape, center, and variability accurate?</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    See graph below. Yes, predictions were accurate.
  </p>
  <image source="3-2/exercise-16.png">
  </image>
</solution>
</activity>


<activity>
 <!--Question 16-->
<statement>
<p>Using what you have seen so far, which is least likely:
<ol>
<li>Randomly picking one word from the Gettysburg Address and finding it be 6 letters or more.</li>
<li>Randomly picking 5 words from the Gettysburg Address and finding the mean of the 5
words to be 6 letters or more in length.</li>
<li>Randomly picking 10 words from the Gettysburg Address and finding the mean of the 10
words to be 6 letters or more in length.</li></ol>
</p>
<p>Explain your reasoning.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Randomly picking 10 words and finding the mean of these words to be 6 letters or more is the
    least likely because as the sample size increases from 1 to 5 to 10 the standard deviation of the
    sampling distribution will be progressively smaller and a value of 6 will become further and
    further in the tail of the distribution as the sample means cluster more closely around 4.295.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

</subsection>

</section>